🏥 Hospital EHR Database Cleanup & Normalization (PostgreSQL)
1. Project Overview

This project transforms raw Synthea-generated EHR data into a production-ready, analytics-oriented PostgreSQL schema.

The original dataset contained:

Inconsistent data types (extensive use of varchar and float)

Uppercase column names requiring quoted identifiers

Missing primary keys

No foreign key constraints

No indexing strategy

The goal was to redesign the schema to meet real-world hospital analytics standards.

2. Data Modeling & Governance Principles
2.1 UUID Standardization

All identifier columns were converted to PostgreSQL native uuid type:

patients.id

encounters.id

providers.id

organizations.id

All related foreign keys (patient_id, encounter_id, provider_id, etc.)

Why UUID?

128-bit globally unique identifier

Type-safe

Smaller storage (16 bytes vs 36-char string)

Efficient indexing

Production-grade distributed system compatibility

2.2 Strong Typing for Date & Time

Clinical timestamps → timestamptz

Birth and death dates → date

Observation timestamps → timestamptz

This ensures:

Time zone safety

Accurate duration calculations (e.g., LOS)

Reliable cohort construction

2.3 Financial Columns Converted to Numeric

All monetary fields were converted from float4/real to:

numeric(12,2)

Examples:

base_encounter_cost

total_claim_cost

payer_coverage

revenue

Reason: Floating point types introduce rounding errors and are not acceptable for financial calculations.

2.4 Column Naming Standardization

All column names were converted to lowercase to eliminate quoted identifiers:

Example:

"DATE" → obs_time

"PATIENT" → patient_id

"ENCOUNTER" → encounter_id

This improves SQL readability and prevents case-sensitivity issues.

3. Schema Improvements by Table
3.1 patients

id → uuid (Primary Key)

birthdate, deathdate → date

zip → varchar(10)

lat/lon → numeric(9,6)

Healthcare cost columns → numeric(12,2)

3.2 encounters (Core Fact Table)

Central fact table in the model.

id → uuid (Primary Key)

patient_id, organization_id, provider_id, payer_id → uuid

start_time, stop_time → timestamptz

Financial columns → numeric(12,2)

Foreign Keys:

patient_id → patients.id

organization_id → organizations.id

provider_id → providers.id

Indexes:

patient_id

start_time

organization_id

provider_id

3.3 conditions

Added condition_id bigserial as Primary Key

patient_id, encounter_id → uuid

start_time, stop_time → timestamptz

Foreign Keys:

patient_id → patients.id

encounter_id → encounters.id

Indexes:

patient_id

encounter_id

start_time

3.4 procedures

Standardized column names

date → timestamptz

base_cost → numeric(12,2)

Added procedure_id bigserial as Primary Key

Foreign Keys:

patient_id → patients.id

encounter_id → encounters.id

3.5 medications

start_time, stop_time → timestamptz

base_cost, payer_coverage, total_cost → numeric(12,2)

Added medication_id bigserial as Primary Key

Foreign Keys:

patient_id → patients.id

encounter_id → encounters.id

3.6 observations

Renamed columns to standardized format

obs_time → timestamptz

patient_id, encounter_id → uuid

Added observation_id bigserial as Primary Key

value intentionally preserved as varchar (mixed numeric/text values)

Foreign Keys:

patient_id → patients.id

encounter_id → encounters.id

Indexes:

patient_id

encounter_id

obs_time

3.7 organizations

id → uuid (Primary Key)

lat/lon → numeric(9,6)

revenue → numeric(12,2)

utilization → integer

Referenced by:

encounters.organization_id

providers.organization_id

3.8 providers

id → uuid (Primary Key)

organization_id → uuid

lat/lon → numeric(9,6)

Foreign Keys:

organization_id → organizations.id

encounters.provider_id → providers.id

4. Referential Integrity Validation

Before adding foreign keys, orphan checks were performed:

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM encounters e
LEFT JOIN patients p ON e.patient_id = p.id
WHERE p.id IS NULL;

All constraints were validated before enforcement.

5. Index Verification

Query plans were validated using:

EXPLAIN ANALYZE
SELECT *
FROM ehr.conditions
WHERE patient_id = 'some_uuid';

Confirmed:

Bitmap Index Scan

Index Scan

Reduced execution time

6. Final Architecture

The resulting model follows a clean fact-centered structure:

patients
    ↓
encounters
    ↓
conditions
procedures
medications
observations

With supporting dimension tables:

organizations

providers

This structure supports:

Volume analysis

Length of stay (LOS)

Readmission rate

Revenue analysis

Provider utilization

Cohort construction

7. Backup & Reproducibility

The schema was exported using:

Full backup (structure + data)

Schema-only backup for version control

This ensures reproducibility and safe experimentation.
